From 06f2d2bdbc61c9f188e81ba6260845234de44cb6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christian Murphy <christian.murphy.42@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 12:10:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] Prove that a request for a link reaches its handler

Lean 4.35.0's standard library proves Nat.toNat?_repr, so String.toNat? reads back what toString prints for a Nat. With it, requestSegments_linkFor shows that a link from linkFor reaches its route's handler with the values passed to linkFor. The proof follows the link through Std.Http's request-target parser and decoder, and through dispatch.

The theorem assumes two properties of Std.Http, ReadsBack and EncodesDigits, since its parser, decoder and encoder have no lemmas. Handler.lean fuzzes both with Plausible. The theorem leaves out a route that starts with an empty literal, because a browser sends its link to another host (#9).

Signed-off-by: Christian Murphy <christian.murphy.42@gmail.com>
