From 75545894ca851505fc55334d1b6418c54e9ee454 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christian Murphy <christian.murphy.42@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 01:15:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] Prove that encoding keeps digits, and narrow ReadsBack
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Add decodedPath, which decodes a request's path for respond? and for Router. requestSegments calls it, so the round trip theorem names the function the server runs.

ReadsBack is now ∀ xs, readsBack xs = true, and Handler.lean fuzzes readsBack itself, so the fuzzing follows any change to the definition. Its guard now leaves out only [""], the one list whose link reads back as something else. A second check starts every list with an empty segment.

encode_repr proves what EncodesDigits assumed, so requestSegments_linkFor no longer needs it. The proof unfolds the encoder and its private push. It also adds a lemma that turns ByteArray.foldl into a list fold.

fits also checks maxUriLength, the H1 parser's limit on a request target.

Signed-off-by: Christian Murphy <christian.murphy.42@gmail.com>
